//
// calls.md `@FR-F-Block` discards a block's value "only where the BLOCK itself is a statement
// — a `;`-terminated one".  A `match` used as a VALUE is not that, so every arm has to produce
// one; an arm that yields nothing leaves the value undefined on that path.
//
// A void arm is exempt from the arm-type unification, and rightly so in STATEMENT position
// (loft#1382's other half).  In VALUE position the exemption let the whole `match` take the
// OTHER arms' type and answer `null` — a value the program never wrote and the type never
// declared — with no diagnostic on `--interpret`, and a raw rustc E0308 naming a generated
// `.rs` file on `--native`.  The `if` twin was refused in the parser all along, so one
// construct answered and the other refused for the same program.
//
// The refusal is what the freeze axis picks: of the two available answers, the accepting one
// is silent-wrong.  It fires in the parser, so the rustc error is no longer reachable.
//
// The STATEMENT cells below are the boundary from the other side and are loft#1382's subject:
// they must keep compiling in either arm order, because there the value is discarded.
//
// @falsified-at: 964bab93 — interpret exit 1 -> 0 and expectations FAIL/3 -> 0/3; native the
//   same.  On the parent build the three cells COMPILE and answer `v=null`, so none of the
//   expected refusals is emitted — which is the defect, stated as a falsification.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees,
//   measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against 964bab93; falsify prints only the
//   channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both. The
//   exit/expectations channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not
//   scored in that run.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: expected integer, got void on a match arm
// @EXPECT_ERROR: expected integer, got void on a match arm
// @EXPECT_ERROR: expected integer, got void on a match arm

fn main() {
  k = 2;

  // VALUE position, the void arm second.
  v1 = match k { 1 => { 5 }, _ => { println("one") } };

  // VALUE position, the void arm FIRST — the mirror, refused for the same reason.
  v2 = match k { 1 => { println("two") }, _ => { 5 } };

  // VALUE position, three arms with one void in the middle.
  v3 = match k { 1 => { 5 }, 2 => { println("three") }, _ => { 7 } };

  println("unreached {v1} {v2} {v3}");
}
